[Music]  hi my name is sandrine huzan and i'm the  stranding and rehabilitation manager for  the sea otter program at the monterey  bay aquarium  come on in and take a sneak peek the  outer care program at the monterey bay  aquarium takes in  stranded injured and orphaned sea otters  to be rehabilitated and released back  into the wild so we actually spend the  majority of the time  observing these otters in this office  using our camera systems and  monitors this is a great tool for us to  be able to observe them  without directly impacting their  behavior these are the current otters  that we have in-house  and as you can tell the majority of them  have numbers for names  and this is so that we don't become  attached to these otters that are going  to be released back into the wild  ivy is one of our resident otters and  she acts as a surrogate for  our orphan pups like all wild animals  it's important for the public to give  them their distance  injured and orphaned otters can be very  dangerous and can fight  they can easily transmit diseases to  humans as well which is why it's  important to  keep your distance and allow for experts  to handle them  paul is prepping some food and what do  we got  we are currently from the squid  this is definitely one of their least  favorite items but it's good for them to  have a variety  sea otters at the monterey bay aquarium  eat very well it is important that they  have a diverse diet  providing proper enrichment is really  important for all  age groups that are under our care but  specifically for pups this gives them  the opportunity to practice manipulating  objects which then  translates to being able to handle live  prey as they get older taking care of  pops is a full-time job  they get offered formula approximately  every three to four hours  and once they get older and have the  appropriate dentition  we then start offering solid food  eventually we get them to the point  that they can recover food and dive to  the bottom on their own  before going in with a surrogate one of  our primary goals when working with  these dependent pups  is ensuring their successful release  back in the wild and one of the ways  that we can do that  is by helping to prevent imprinting and  habituation on humans we want to make  sure  that they don't associate anything  positive with humans such as food  or grooming in order to do that we need  to disguise ourselves  to create a shapeless form that hides  the human body  so since this heart is going to be  released back into the wild  i want to make sure to disguise myself  properly so that she's not associating  anything positive with humans  and warm formula is definitely something  positive  so i'm going to be wearing this disguise  first this  then a mask cover my face which i'll  have down once i'm in with her  and then i also want a way of protecting  my arms because  like we talked about these otters have  sharp teeth  once a pup has been weaned from their  surrogate they will spend more time in a  tank  with different companion animals working  on socialization  continuing to learn how to forage  independently and  at the point in which we see that they  are consistent in their behavior  body condition looks good code condition  looks good and around  nine to ten months is really the time  when we would start considering  releasing  given that we've spent so much time  working with these otters and  rehabilitating them  and having gone through the surrogacy  program we want to make sure that  there's a way that we can track them  and monitor their progress once they're  released back into the wild so before  they go  they are outfitted with some of these  accessories the first thing that they  get  is a radio transmitter that is  surgically implanted in their abdomen  and this transmitter will be able to  emit a signal  every time they're at the surface of the  water and if we have an antenna  and a receiver we can find them and hear  them when they're foraging or grooming  at the surface of the water  the other thing that they will get are  flipper tags combination of two  flipper tags one on each flipper so that  we can visually identify them  when they're out in the water  [Music]  okay  [Music]  [Applause]  visit the monterey bay aquarium's  website montereybayaquarium.org to learn  more about how you can donate  [Music]  you

